From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] backup-log: stop holding the shutdown open for the whole
 journal

Shutting down or restarting from the power menu takes about a minute and a
half on tissot, nearly all of it spent in one place: at the end of the
teardown systemd sat for 71 seconds with nothing to show for it and then
logged "backup-log.service: Stopping timed out. Terminating." Everything
behind it - the remaining units, the unmounts, reboot.target itself - waited
that out. Two separate things put it there.

The work is unbounded. save-journald-logs.py runs "journalctl -a", with no
boot filter, so it walks every boot journald still has: on this device that
is a 1.2 GB persistent journal, 36 seconds to render and ~180 MB of text
written back into /var/log. It then tars and gzips all of /var/log into
/var/spool/rdxd/previous_boot_logs.tar.gz - including /var/log/journal, so
the same 1.2 GB gets compressed again, plus the dump just written. Limiting
the dump to the boot that is ending and keeping journald's own database out
of the tarball leaves the backup doing what it is for: a readable copy of the
logs from the boot that just finished. The earlier boots have not gone
anywhere; they are still in /var/log/journal where journalctl -b -1 reads
them.

The ordering is unstated. The unit declares Conflicts=shutdown.target and
nothing else, which says the two may not both be up but says nothing about
which job runs first. So the ExecStop= was being scheduled alongside the
filesystem teardown rather than before it, which is why /var was already
being unmounted while the backup was still writing to it - "umount: /var:
target is busy", then the backup killed part-way through. Before=shutdown.target
is what pairs with Conflicts=, and RequiresMountsFor= keeps the stop job ahead
of the unmount, since units are stopped in reverse start order.

TimeoutStopSec=30s is the backstop: a log backup is a convenience, and a
shutdown the user asked for should not wait on it indefinitely if some future
change makes it slow again.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
diff --git a/common/lib/systemd/system/backup-log.service b/common/lib/systemd/system/backup-log.service
index 92773cb..754a85d 100644
--- a/common/lib/systemd/system/backup-log.service
+++ b/common/lib/systemd/system/backup-log.service
@@ -17,9 +17,21 @@
 [Unit]
 Description=webos-initscripts - "%n"
 Conflicts=shutdown.target
+# Conflicts= on its own says the unit and shutdown.target may not both be up,
+# but says nothing about the order the two jobs run in. Without Before= the
+# ExecStop= below can be scheduled alongside the filesystem teardown, which is
+# how it came to be writing into /var/log while var.mount was being stopped:
+# the unmount failed with "target is busy" and the backup was killed part-way.
+Before=shutdown.target
+# Everything this writes lives under /var. Ordering after the mount keeps the
+# stop job ahead of the unmount, since units stop in reverse start order.
+RequiresMountsFor=/var/log /var/spool
 
 [Service]
 Type=oneshot
 ExecStart=-/bin/touch /tmp/backup-log-ready
 ExecStop=/lib/systemd/system/scripts/save-journald-logs.py
 RemainAfterExit=yes
+# A log backup is a convenience, not a reason to hold up a shutdown the user
+# asked for. Anything slower than this gets abandoned rather than waited on.
+TimeoutStopSec=30s
diff --git a/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/save-journald-logs.py b/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/save-journald-logs.py
index 0f882fa..5711925 100755
--- a/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/save-journald-logs.py
+++ b/common/lib/systemd/system/scripts/save-journald-logs.py
@@ -29,13 +29,28 @@ def remove_journald_old_files():
         os.remove(f)
 
 def flush_journald_log_to_file():
-    journalctl_options = ['-a']
+    # -b limits the dump to the boot that is ending. Without it this walks
+    # every boot journald still has on disk, which on a device with a
+    # persistent journal is the whole 1+ GB of it: 36 seconds to produce and
+    # ~180 MB written to flash, on every single shutdown. The older boots are
+    # still in /var/log/journal for journalctl -b -1 and friends to read, so
+    # nothing is lost by leaving them out of this text copy.
+    journalctl_options = ['-a', '-b']
     command = ['journalctl'] + journalctl_options
     log_file_name = "/var/log/journald-" + get_timestamp() + ".log"
     with open(log_file_name, "w") as logFile:
         call(command, stdout=logFile)
     return
 
+def exclude_journal(tarinfo):
+    # /var/log/journal is journald's own binary database, and it is persistent
+    # here: gzipping it into the backup means compressing a copy of every boot
+    # ever recorded, alongside the text dump that was just made of the last
+    # one. The text dump is what this backup is for.
+    if tarinfo.name == 'log/journal' or tarinfo.name.startswith('log/journal/'):
+        return None
+    return tarinfo
+
 def backup_var_log():
     rdxdDir = "/var/spool/rdxd/"
     if not os.path.isdir(rdxdDir):
@@ -46,7 +61,7 @@ def backup_var_log():
 
     source = '/var/log/'
     with tarfile.open(saveFile, "w:gz") as tar:
-        tar.add(source, arcname='log')
+        tar.add(source, arcname='log', filter=exclude_journal)
     return
 
 if __name__ == '__main__':
